#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2f03be7e-0a77-5869-b591-6a8f8f3942de" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5814" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the equation of the polynomial above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/d979178b10acbe0ddfed255dad253c61.png)\n\n1. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n2. $y=(x+2)^3(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n3. $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2$, $x=-1$, and $x=2$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x =-2$, this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x =-1$, this root has even multiplicity.\n- Since the graph passes through $x = 2$, this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2$, the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is\n\n$$\ny= (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128898 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03ac24f-82a6-5af8-b1b8-9021e521d47a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e158501f-0bac-5d16-956a-59da7f82620f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5818" :question/problem "Which of the above could be the graph of $y = (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/4ca45f72e9238f127f4f2fb3f6072708.png)" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=\\pm 2$ is $1$, which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2$, which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\textbf{I}$ and $\\textbf{III}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, $y$ approaches $\\infty$.\n\nOnly graph $\\textbf{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is **$\\textbf{III}$**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "The graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/9cfc8c5b4d8ae817c96c52ab32266bc9.png)\n\n1. $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n2. $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n3. $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty$." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty$, the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "53aa15a0-8633-51b8-8731-9099eec94940" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9941" :question/problem "A part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/ead16b30a111439d43b0f0b8024f158c.png)\n\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity\n- $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity\n- $x=-4$ is a simple root" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$<br><br>(Simple Root) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/8d4fa6772f455694bd3de3902a20aeeb.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/70231eae4f8dd6d1843bad449fc2e7c3.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple Root)<br><br>Even | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/858c9a8ff00003e7465a1ff3ed7c096b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/53e2a4b825235fd256356c80c61c67cd.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple Root)<br><br>Odd | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/3d1237ff3984c61c337993fffbfe9a3b.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/b341a19379dc575c8b225b2b99d79689.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a$, we have a *simple* root $x=a$.\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a$, we have a *multiple* root $x=a$. Furthermore:If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a$, the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*. If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a$, the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-4$. So, $x=-4$ is a *multiple* root.\n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-4$. So, $x=-4$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03ac24f-82a6-5af8-b1b8-9021e521d47a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Factored Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84210ced-aa4a-5536-ac7f-9e51ea6b0bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127515} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e6589c-a85a-5804-9258-4a5c15711c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127516} {:answer/id #uuid "e5970b42-7bca-56ca-bee3-d3a6f45b40f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given graphs are correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127517} {:answer/id #uuid "05630770-9a91-53fc-b8a1-515a7c4781ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127518} {:answer/id #uuid "72a0fcad-45a7-5a28-a089-a5576661b8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e6589c-a85a-5804-9258-4a5c15711c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ffa8b15-dabc-57c0-ab85-7cdca8eeaa7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127514}] :question/id #uuid "c0deda6d-d55e-5ff9-9b25-84b2554afbce" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-38630" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2049/Source/Images/q-38630.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y = x(x + 1)(x - 1)$?"}] [